---

A codec turns CapTP messages into bytes and back. The session decides whether
a reference is valid, so changing codecs changes only the wire format.

glowcap ships two:

| Codec | Use it when | Tradeoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) | Always, unless a measured glowcap-to-glowcap workload says otherwise | Compact and interoperable. Finding an arbitrary field requires a scan |
| [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) | Both peers are glowcap and large or partially read payloads benefit from fixed offsets | Constant-time field access. Larger frames and no outside interoperability |

[One message, two encodings. Syrup lays it out as variable-length byte records and Cap'n Proto as fixed 8-byte words. Decode either and you get back the same message.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-codec.svg'))

# Syrup

Syrup is the OCapN format and glowcap's default. It is the only codec glowcap
uses with [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and other
implementations.

Its length-prefixed, variable-width records keep small control messages
compact. The decoder can find a frame boundary and decode it in one forward
pass. Random access is the tradeoff: reaching item *i* requires walking the
records before it.

Syrup is also glowcap's signing format. The signature on a peer's announced
location and the certificates that authorize a
[three-party handoff]($link.page('protocol')) are made over canonical Syrup
bytes, even when two glowcap peers exchange their messages with Cap'n Proto.

# Cap'n Proto

Cap'n Proto lays messages out in 8-byte words with fixed field offsets. That
makes partial reads and random access cheap, but padding and pointer words make
small messages larger.

Goblins does not speak this encoding, so use Syrup for Goblins interoperability. Codec
and CapTP version are separate choices. glowcap's own profile pairs Cap'n
Proto with `glowcap-next`, which tracks newer draft operations. Cap'n Proto
can also carry CapTP 1.0 between glowcap peers. Set
`codec = .capnp` and the version you need in Zig `SessionOptions`, or use the
matching Host or binding preference.

# Choosing

Measure your own messages before switching. The
[performance page]($link.page('performance')) measures one small
mixed-argument call, which may look nothing like yours.

Either codec can hand the session a temporary view of a message. glowcap copies
into owned memory only when it must keep that message after decode finishes.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains how peers
negotiate the CapTP version and codec. The
[architecture doc](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)
shows where a Zig program can plug in another codec.
